INT. FINCH & DAUGHTERS PIANO MOVERS - TRUCK BED - DAY

A grand piano wrapped in blankets and straps, wedged against a ramp. Daylight spills in the open back.

BARRY FINCH, 50s, sweating through a company polo, pushes from behind. LOLA SANTANGELO, 30s, in work gloves, pulls from the front. KENJI MORI, 20s, the new hire, stands on the ramp holding a clipboard and no idea.

BARRY
On three. One. Two --

LOLA
Not on three. After three.

BARRY
That's what I said.

KENJI
You said "on."

BARRY
Thank you, Kenji.

They heave. The piano rolls three feet and stops. Barry slips; Lola grabs the strap; the piano rocks. They both freeze as it teeters and then settles.

LOLA
(whispering)
Nobody breathe.

BARRY
I'm breathing.

LOLA
Breathe quieter.

GLORIA DUPREE, 60s, the customer, in a silk robe and curlers, strides up the driveway with a pastry box.

GLORIA
Is that my Steinway?

KENJI
Yes, ma'am.

GLORIA
It's backward.

Barry and Lola look at each other. Both groan. Kenji drops the clipboard. It clatters down the ramp, and the piano, as if answering, begins to roll.

LOLA
Kenji!

BARRY
Catch it!

All three dive. The piano stops two inches from Gloria's slippers. She opens the pastry box and offers a donut.

GLORIA
Anyone?
